  "project_id": 7898047,
  "title": "wiretap: guard wtap_file_size/wtap_fstat/wtap_get_compression_type against NULL file handles",
  "description": "These public accessors all use the pattern (wth-\\>fh == NULL) ? wth-\\>random_fh : wth-\\>fh to pick a usable file handle, but they don't check the result. After wtap_sequential_close() sets wth-\\>fh = NULL on a non-seekable source (pipe/stdin), wth-\\>random_fh is also NULL because random access can't be opened on a pipe (see file_access.c WTAP_ERR_RANDOM_OPEN_PIPE), so both ends of the ternary are NULL.\n\nwtap_file_size and wtap_fstat then call file_fstat(NULL, ...) which dereferences stream-\\>fd. wtap_get_compression_type calls file_get_compression_type(NULL) which dereferences stream-\\>is_compressed.\n\nNo existing in-tree caller hits this — they all call these functions while a handle is still open — but external plugins and language bindings that query a wtap after sequential close are exposed. The issue was found while testing a candidate WSLua feature (get_capture_file_info()), where the new entry point is reachable from a Lua tap draw() callback after sequential close on a pipe capture; the underlying bug pre-dates that feature and is fixed independently here.\n\nReturn -1 with errno=EBADF from the fstat-based functions and WS_FILE_UNKNOWN_COMPRESSION from the compression query when both handles are NULL. All existing callers already treat -1 as an error return, so no caller changes are required.\n\nAI-Assisted: yes (Claude)",
