    {
      "id": "34b0594f0779e9a539484fce8ffda9d7372cc38c",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 3379713543,
          "type": null,
          "body": "> Yes, code isn't currently reachable.\n\nI think the only way you can have a still-valid `wtap` with neither `fh` nor `random_fh` being non-null is if `wtap_fdclose()` has been called.\n\n(And `wtap_fdclose()` should set the closed handle members to `NULL`.)",
          "author": {
            "id": 5636682,
            "username": "guyharris",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Guy Harris",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/1b7c243a9b1d6587a9a9067e5d83aba1e87212df7c4eeae3690f791ecbe4675a?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/guyharris"
          },
          "created_at": "2026-05-24T07:51:41.920Z",
          "updated_at": "2026-05-24T07:51:41.920Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 488252169,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": false,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 25047,
          "commands_changes": {}
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "0254eb117b464030e60f61cf9b51fdc968c3ab5e",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 3379863539,
          "type": null,
          "body": "> And `wtap_fdclose()` should set the closed handle members to `NULL`.\n\nNo, because `wtap_fdclose()` is a ***VERY*** specialized hack for a ***VERY*** specialized problem, namely that Windows doesn't - or, at the time, didn't - allow renaming of open files. The idea is that the code *temporarily* closes the file descriptor, does the rename, and then reopens the file with its new name and puts the new descriptor back into the `wtap`.\n\nIt may be that the only versions of Windows that we currently support (and maybe Windows 10 too, if we only support Windows 11), allow renaming of open files if you ask Windows nicely, in which case we may be able to nuke that hack.",
          "author": {
            "id": 5636682,
            "username": "guyharris",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Guy Harris",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/1b7c243a9b1d6587a9a9067e5d83aba1e87212df7c4eeae3690f791ecbe4675a?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/guyharris"
          },
